{
  "dom_id": "question-159639",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "It is known that the weights of oranges from farms X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=7$ grams and ${\\sigma }_{y}=8$ grams, respectively. The owner of the farms wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of oranges from farm X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of oranges from farm Y. He decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $25$ oranges from farm X and $20$ oranges from farm Y, finding that their mean weights are $125$ grams and $128.2$ grams, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
